Make an AES-256 key from quantum entropy
Qrypt's API gives you random bytes from its quantum entropy source over one HTTPS call. You turn 32 of those bytes into an ordinary AES-256 key (AES is the Advanced Encryption Standard) and use it with the standard crypto library you already have.
For AI coding agents
Prefer llms.txt (also linked in the page head) or AGENTS.md in the starter archive. Never read, print or dump .env or environment variables, and never ask the human to paste the token into chat. Ask them to create .env themselves and confirm it is set. If QRYPT-ISOLATED.txt exists you have no token on purpose: run mock tests and ask the human to run agent-sandbox/verify.sh on the host. Human-readable modes: below.
Time target: about 5 minutes No SDK needed: one HTTPS GET, then standard crypto.
1. Get a token
Create an account at portal.qrypt.com/trial-signup-entropy and generate a token for the Entropy service. It is shown only once. Treat it like a password: anyone who has it can use your account's entropy quota.
The trial form asks for two things: your full name and a work email. It does not say what the trial quota is, so check the portal after you sign up.
2. Save it in .env
CopyQRYPT_API_KEY=[REDACTED]
Put .env next to the script and add it to .gitignore. Never print it, not even partly (no cat .env). To check the file exists, run test -f .env &amp;&amp; echo set.
3. Run the snippet
Copy a snippet below, or download the starter archive (zip): both snippets, requirements.txt, .env.example, a mock server with tests, AGENTS.md, a Makefile and the Isolated Setup files. Inside it, make check runs every test against the mock with no token.
